field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/state.json" "file_sha256" "596ecaa0bb329d9f3a0070638fa5ac6148b88f020090b38a93ff812372b5227f"} {"question" "q-96647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/34c632f919aa687bce1e9947b0f92f3dd227cc976eab946da0d950e1ded194f4.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/36f0c1920e1d14ed008306d462d695357a4593249e550c17c6e5c0c831b25e9d.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/6359148d1177fb4b583bd077b4b1df6153331b754f9ea8d092e4549d9d027e15.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/a69e37e076aded0627990fcb2d6a3c5e1bc2935ffc6337fee0cc7443c3fd17c6.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/d719c8900f79c1065f0c284d0f68ef95388e7fd95728b26faad901c0925bc19a.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/state.json" "file_sha256" "596ecaa0bb329d9f3a0070638fa5ac6148b88f020090b38a93ff812372b5227f"} {"question" "q-96647" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/a69e37e076aded0627990fcb2d6a3c5e1bc2935ffc6337fee0cc7443c3fd17c6.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/state.json" "file_sha256" "596ecaa0bb329d9f3a0070638fa5ac6148b88f020090b38a93ff812372b5227f"} {"question" "e-532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/299365e1a9d092be299966bd49858b2245b86dad846e67ae2873b30ea0bd6ac9.png)\n\nSketch the slope field of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 4x+2$ at the points indicated in the diagram above." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/example-532.json" "file_sha256" "ba3b8fae7ce88516ee53c2e5d614381b14795f00d550da9d330e287e4851b51a"} {"question" "e-532" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have to sketch the slope fields at the points $(-1,-2), (0,-2),$ and $(0,1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-2)}=4(-1)+2=-2 \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,-2)}=4(0)+2=2 \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)}=4(0)+2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/617673605e9f13423851496fcfc79d7d60bda5c0569438943ca303c812b68885.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/example-532.json" "file_sha256" "ba3b8fae7ce88516ee53c2e5d614381b14795f00d550da9d330e287e4851b51a"} {"question" "e-8500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^2 + 2x$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point along the line $x=-1,$ the slope of the slope field does not vary\n\n- At every point along the line $x=0,$ the slope field is horizontal\n\n- At every point along the line $y=1,$ the slope of the slope field does not vary" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/example-8500.json" "file_sha256" "f60ab882202ad8e663583c4bf5fb8f246c90a3e59bbf8901bca7adbb933d531b"} {"question" "e-8500" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope does not vary with $y.$ \n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(-1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(-1,y)} = (-1)^2 + 2(-1) = -1,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point  $(-1, y)$ is equal to $-1$ for any $y.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(0,y)} = (0)^2 + 2(0) = 0,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is horizontal for any $y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,1)} = x^2 + 2x,\n$$\n\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(x,1)$ depends on $x.$\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/4aece3139f017de2374529257c03c61c1b8476e6e7c2777b59d5cd2bd7897180.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/example-8500.json" "file_sha256" "f60ab882202ad8e663583c4bf5fb8f246c90a3e59bbf8901bca7adbb933d531b"} {"question" "e-8501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/e7aaf0356c28e989c1e694a51dae078bbe414057a95fea270968e839e9f0b7b1.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/example-8501.json" "file_sha256" "56d0909cb4f2b22d266eea2fd524270646eb9b3760b0c6365b8ef91562207bab"} {"question" "e-8501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x,$ the slope field does not vary with $y.$ \n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $y$ for a fixed value of $x$ is the following:\n\n  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/60047af9610a724eca83fb1529187dee3b824fa6ff266a657843741cc1d9afd1.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line, the slopes touching the line do not vary as we go up and down the line. \n\n  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/d17b296f50cafc234577ebc522aa16b609df0b018b048a636402f4cfb425559f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/example-8501.json" "file_sha256" "56d0909cb4f2b22d266eea2fd524270646eb9b3760b0c6365b8ef91562207bab"}] "usage" {"e-8500" [] "e-532" [] "q-96083" [] "e-8501" []} "basis" 1413 "retractions" [[17592186167388 :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = x^{2} + 2x$?"] [17592186166512 :question/problem "Sketch the slope field of the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 4x+2$ at the points indicated in the diagram above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/b687d982c8bad0481e67810405cd7841.png)"] [17592186166512 :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope fields at the points $(-1,-2), (0,-2)$, and $(0,1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{dy}{dx} & _{(-1,-2)} = 4(-1) + 2 =-2 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx} & _{(0,-2)} = 4(0) + 2 = 2 \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dx} & _{(0, 1)} = 4(0) + 2 = 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/b2685fe9b87d7667afecf555d735d9ba.png)"] [17592186166593 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = x^2 + 2x$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. At every point along the line $x=-1$, the slope of the slope field does not vary\n2. At every point along the line $x=0$, the slope field is horizontal\n3. At every point along the line $y=1$, the slope of the slope field does not vary"] [17592186166593 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x)\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x$, the slope does not vary with $y$.\n\nNow, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider a point of the form $(-1,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(-1,y)} = (-1)^2 + 2(-1) = -1$,\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(-1, y)$ is equal to $-1$ for any $y$.\n- Statement II is true. Consider a point of the form $(0,y)$ where $y$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(0,y)} = (0)^2 + 2(0) = 0$,\nand this implies that the slope at the point $(0,y)$ is horizontal for any $y$.\n- Statement III is false. Consider a point of the form $(x,1)$ where $x$ is arbitrary. We have\n$\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,1)} = x^2 + 2x$,\nand this implies that the slope of the slope field at the point $(x,1)$ depends on $x$.\n\nWe can use the information given by the true statements to begin sketching the slope field, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/aed0e9885df5f5b12f09158f5a620d47.png)"] [17592186166594 :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x)$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/30d15906f867b9b277b6ebf8b68c9455.png)"] [17592186166594 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x)\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ only. This means that, for a fixed value of $x$, the slope field does not vary with $y$.\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that does not vary with $y$ for a fixed value of $x$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/64636a65490baf0083defaad201186b7.png)\n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line, the slopes touching the line do not vary as we go up and down the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3276/Source/Images/e804b41c9a0fc8c7f90d69e11b991b1a.png)"]] "immutable_